arXiv:2510.22475v1 [cs.CL] 26 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document"><img src="2510.22475v1/CHOIR_nocolor.png" id="g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:14/14;" width="14" height="14" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"> CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiangjue Dong
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Cong Wang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Maria Teleki
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Millennium Bismay
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">James Caverlee
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Texas A&amp;M University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{xj.dong, congwang, mariateleki, mbismay, caverlee}@tamu.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Persona-assigned Large Language Models (LLMs) can adopt diverse roles, enabling personalized and context-aware reasoning. However, even minor demographic perturbations in personas, such as simple pronoun changes, can alter reasoning trajectories, leading to divergent sets of correct answers. Instead of treating these variations as biases to be mitigated, we explore their potential as a constructive resource to improve reasoning robustness.
We propose CHOIR (Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness), a test-time framework that harmonizes multiple persona-conditioned reasoning signals into a unified prediction. CHOIR orchestrates a collaborative decoding process among counterfactual personas, dynamically balancing agreement and divergence in their reasoning paths.
Experiments on various reasoning benchmarks demonstrate that CHOIR consistently enhances performance across demographics, model architectures, scales, and tasks – without additional training. Improvements reach up to 26.4% for individual demographic groups and 19.2% on average across five demographics. It remains effective even when base personas are suboptimal. By reframing persona variation as a constructive signal, CHOIR provides a scalable and generalizable approach to more reliable LLM reasoning.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.22475v1/fig2.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/299;" width="471" height="299" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S0.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM performance is sensitive to simple pronoun perturbations.</span> This figure shows accuracy variations of the Llama-1B model on GSM8K across five different base personas, each perturbed with <span id="S0.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">he</span>, <span id="S0.F1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">she</span>, and <span id="S0.F1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">they</span> pronouns. Even when overall accuracies are similar (pairs i, ii, iii), the sets of correctly solved problems differ (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Personas for <math id="S0.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\text{Gender}}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mtext>Gender</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\text{Gender}}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> are listed in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; detailed analysis is in §<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Simple Demographic Perturbation Impacts Performance ‣ 2 Uncovering Complementary Knowledge in Counterfactual Personas ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Persona-assigned Large Language Models (LLMs) can improve zero-shot reasoning by steering the model to generate personalized, in-character responses with detailed reasoning traces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tseng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. However, persona assignment also introduces challenges: it can amplify biases, produce unsafe outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, or expose deep-rooted biases inherent in LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. These risks highlight the need for careful implementation and improved methods to detect and mitigate biases, particularly given the limited success of existing techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To better understand model sensitivity to demographic cues embedded within persona prompts, we construct a set of counterfactual gender personas by perturbing demographic terms. Our analysis reveals two key findings: (1) even simple demographic perturbations can significantly affect model performance (Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), echoing recent findings on persona-induced bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al. (2024)</a></cite>; and (2) while overall accuracy remains similar, different persona framings lead to divergent sets of correct answers (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). These findings indicate that even small perturbations in personas can lead to different reasoning trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While much of the existing work treats such variation as a form of bias to be diagnosed and mitigated, we take a different perspective: <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Can demographic perturbations be used constructively to support more robust reasoning?</span>
To explore this question, we introduce CHOIR (Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness), a test-time framework that enhances reasoning by harmonizing multiple persona-conditioned reasoning paths obtained from diverse counterfactual demographic framing. CHOIR’s dynamic weighting mechanism integrates these complementary signals by giving more influence to paths that show strong agreement with the rest of the ensemble, allowing the model to leverage diverse perspectives without overcommitting to any single one.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">CHOIR operates entirely at inference time and requires no additional training. Experiments across diverse reasoning datasets, model architectures, and scales show that it consistently enhances both accuracy and robustness over baselines, achieving up to 26.4% improvement for individual demographic groups and an average of 19.2% across five demographics (<span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gender</span>, <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Race</span>, <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Religion</span>, <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Disability</span>, and <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Age</span>). Importantly, CHOIR remains effective even when base personas are noisy or suboptimal and generalizes well across demographics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These results indicate that persona variation, when handled systematically, can serve as a rich resource rather than a liability. CHOIR provides a simple and general framework for decoding-time collaboration among demographic framings, opening up new directions for socially aware, reliable, and robust LLM reasoning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.22475v1/fig3.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/148;" width="471" height="148" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Similar overall accuracy reveals complementary reasoning strengths.</span> This figure shows the overlap in correct answers for three pairs of personas from Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with nearly identical accuracy. For example, in (i), the <span id="S1.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">he</span> and <span id="S1.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">she</span> personas differ by only 0.1% in accuracy yet share 315 correct answers, with 73 and 74 uniquely solved, respectively. These non-overlapping sets demonstrate that minimal perturbations can lead to distinct reasoning paths (See §<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Similar Accuracy, Divergent Reasoning ‣ 2 Uncovering Complementary Knowledge in Counterfactual Personas ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> for details.)</figcaption>
</figure>
