<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><span id="S4.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance of CHOIR across five demographic personas (<span id="S4.T3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gender</span>, <span id="S4.T3.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Race</span>, <span id="S4.T3.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Religion</span>, <span id="S4.T3.7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Disability</span>, and <span id="S4.T3.7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Age</span>) on arithmetic and commonsense reasoning datasets.</span> The “Average” column reports mean accuracy (%) across all demographic personas. Results are averaged over three runs with different random seeds; standard deviations are provided in the Appendix. <span id="S4.T3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> values indicate the best performance, and <sup id="S4.T3.9" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup> marks statistically significant improvements (<math id="S4.T3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>) based on paired <span id="S4.T3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span>-tests across demographic scores. Subscript values show gains over the strongest baseline. CHOIR enhances accuracy and exhibits harmonized reasoning across variations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines and implementation details.</span>
Our main comparisons (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) include three representative baselines: (i) Zero-Shot Chain-of-Thought (ZS-CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, where the model is prompted with “Let’s think step by step” to elicit reasoning; (ii) Persona<sub id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sub">average</sub>, which averages the predictions of persona-assigned LLMs across different demographic variants; and (iii) Persona<sub id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sub">majority</sub>, which aggregates predictions by majority vote. We follow the demographic attributes from Table <a href="#S1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. For our main experiments, each primary persona is perturbed along <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gender</span>, <span id="S4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Race</span>, and <span id="S4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Religion</span> (three variants each), and along <span id="S4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Disability</span> and <span id="S4.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Age</span> (two variants each).
A robustness analysis on the number of attributes is provided in the Appendix (Figure <a href="#A5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix E Robustness to the Choice of Demographic Attributes ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
In cases where majority voting produces ties (e.g., three different answers), we randomly select one outcome. To ensure robustness, we report the mean and standard deviation across three runs with different random seeds. We use greedy decoding with a temperature of 0, following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. For smaller models (below 3B), we use a maximum of 512 tokens to improve reasoning capacity, while larger models (3B and above) use 256 tokens. For our main CHOIR experiments, we set the knowledge weight <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{(0)}=n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{(0)}=n-1</annotation></semantics></math> to balance its influence against the <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> counterfactual personas, ensuring that the aggregated demographic variants do not overwhelm the pre-trained reasoning. This ensures a strong and consistent setting for comparison, particularly when model-specific tuning is not feasible. For all individual personas, we set <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{(i)}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{(i)}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. We then conduct a model-specific analysis of the <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> in §<a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 RQ3: What are the key components driving CHOIR’s success? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>. Additional baselines, such as Zero-Shot prompting, Few-Shot CoT, Role-Play prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, and Self-Consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022c</a>)</cite> are evaluated on GSM8K with Llama-8B and presented in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Comparison with Additional Baselines ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
