ath></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -69.32 155.66)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="13.84"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F3.pic4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -76.23 219.7)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="20.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.F3.pic4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><clipPath id="pgfcp4"><path d="M -50.59 0 L 219.22 0 L 219.22 224.16 L -50.59 224.16 Z"></path></clipPath><g clip-path="url(#pgfcp4)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#4D4DFF;" fill="#4D4DFF"><path d="M -9.69 0 h 8.3 v 27.7 h -8.3 Z M 24.04 0 h 8.3 v 92 h -8.3 Z M 57.77 0 h 8.3 v 91.59 h -8.3 Z M 91.49 0 h 8.3 v 110.54 h -8.3 Z M 125.22 0 h 8.3 v 92.8 h -8.3 Z M 158.95 0 h 8.3 v 124.67 h -8.3 Z"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFA64D;" fill="#FFA64D"><path d="M 1.38 0 h 8.3 v 65.17 h -8.3 Z M 35.11 0 h 8.3 v 123.35 h -8.3 Z M 68.84 0 h 8.3 v 133.31 h -8.3 Z M 102.56 0 h 8.3 v 127.55 h -8.3 Z M 136.29 0 h 8.3 v 126.75 h -8.3 Z M 170.02 0 h 8.3 v 160.79 h -8.3 Z"></path></g><g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 65.48 -42.05)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.69em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="37.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.F3.pic4.7" class="ltx_text">Model</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 -89.19 70.55)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="83.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.F3.pic4.8" class="ltx_text">Accuracy (%)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 31.01 240.04)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7.71em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="106.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.F3.pic4.9" class="ltx_text">CommonsenseQA</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></svg></span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S5.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance of CHOIR vs. Persona<sub id="S5.F3.5.1" class="ltx_sub">Majority</sub> across four reasoning datasets.</span> Bars represent average accuracy across <span id="S5.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">five demographic attributes</span> for Llama and Qwen models.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>RQ1: Does CHOIR improve reasoning over strong baselines?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> provide a clear affirmative answer, demonstrating that CHOIR’s improvements are evident in three key ways:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A “Floor-Raising” Effect: Elevating Weaker Baselines.</span> CHOIR can generate strong results even when the individual personas are relatively weak. This is most evident in scenarios where the baselines struggle. For instance, on CommonsenseQA with Llama-8B, the average persona performance is a low 55.47%, which is even lower than ZS-CoT (67.81%), indicating significant confusion among the individual personas. CHOIR synthesizes these weaker signals into a strong result of 71.63%, a +16.16 point absolute improvement. This shows that CHOIR’s harmonization is not a simple average but an intelligent fusion that can identify and amplify the correct reasoning signal even among conflicting outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A “Ceiling-Pushing” Effect: Optimizing Strong Baselines.</span> CHOIR is not only for remedial cases; it also excels at optimizing already strong performance. On MultiArith with Llama-8B, where the Persona<sub id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sub">majority</sub> baseline is already a high 83.98%. CHOIR pushes accuracy even further to 93.13%, achieving a +9.16 point improvement over the strongest baseline. This demonstrates that even when a simple ensemble method like majority voting is effective, CHOIR’s dynamic, logit-level weighting can extract additional signal to solve more complex problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Enhanced Robustness and Stability.</span> Beyond accuracy, CHOIR improves reasoning reliability by addressing the instability and failure modes of baselines: <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First, it mitigates performance variance and sensitivity to demographic terms.</span> The Persona<sub id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_sub">average</sub> often exhibits high variance across demographic groups (e.g., <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>5.3 for disability using Llama3.1-8B-Instruct on GSM8K). As noted by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al. (2024)</a></cite>, LLMs perform worse for certain personas, such as “disabled”. However, our findings reveal that replacing “disabled” with “able-bodied” performed even worse, possibly due to its training data imbalances and associative priors developed during pre-training or post-training and the sensitivity of Persona-Assigned LLMs to minor perturbations analyzed in §<a href="#S2" title="2 Uncovering Complementary Knowledge in Counterfactual Personas ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. From this aspect, we can see that CHOIR enhances performance regardless of the Persona-Assigned LLM’s original capability and mitigates the model’s sensitivity towards certain demographics, confirming its robustness and effectiveness. <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second, CHOIR resolves ambiguous outcomes from simpler ensembles.</span> The Persona<sub id="S5.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_sub">majority</sub> is also prone to failure when reasoning paths are only a few and diverge completely, resulting in a high number of indecisive “tied votes” where no consensus can be reached. By contrast, CHOIR’s dynamic fusion can produce a definitive answer, enhancing its overall reliability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>RQ2: Does CHOIR’s Advantage Generalize?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis confirms that CHOIR is a broadly generalizable method whose advantages are maintained or amplified as model capability increases. We break down this analysis into two parts: broad generalization across various contexts and a deeper analysis of performance scaling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Broad Generalization Across Tasks, Models, Demographics, and Prompt Templates:</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, CHOIR’s effectiveness is not limited to a single domain or model family. It delivers significant average improvements across different tasks and is robust across Llama, Qwen, and Mistral architectures and various demographics (Gender, Race, Religion, Disability, and Age). In addition, CHOIR is robust to the specific wording of the persona instruction. When applying alternative persona instructions, such as “Adopt the identity of {persona}. Answer the questions while staying in strict accordance with the nature of this identity.” (as shown in Table <a href="#A2.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ B.2 Prompting Details ‣ Appendix B More Implementation Details ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), CHOIR still improves the Llama-1B model’s performance to 38.9%, outperforming the baselines. This demonstrates that CHOIR is robust to different persona instructions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gains Maintained or Amplified with Model Capability:</span> A detailed analysis of CHOIR’s performance relative to the strong Persona<sub id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sub">majority</sub> baseline across model families and scales (Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) reveals key trends in CHOIR’s effectiveness:
<span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Consistent Superiority:</span> CHOIR outperforms the strong Persona<sub id="S5.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_sub">majority</sub> baseline in the vast majority of cases across all four datasets and the 24 specific model configurations tested. This consistent advantage confirms that CHOIR’s dynamic, logit-level harmonization is more effective than a simple voting ensemble.
<span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Maintained or Scaled Advantage:</span> On CommonsenseQA, for example, the lead over the baseline for Llama models grows from 11.7 to 13.0 points (1B to 8B), and for Qwen models, it more than doubles from 5.3 to 11.3 points (1.5B to 7B). This demonstrates that CHOIR is a highly scalable method that effectively leverages the increased reasoning capabilities of larger models.
<span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) A Nuanced “Capability Threshold”:</span> On the GSM8K dataset, while the simpler majority vote is competitive on smaller Qwen models, CHOIR re-establishes a commanding lead of over 7 points (64.96% vs. 57.27%) at the 7B scale. This indicates that as models cross a certain capability threshold, CHOIR’s fusion mechanism becomes increasingly crucial for unlocking their better performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>RQ3: What are the key components driving CHOIR’s success?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ablation studies (Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.3 RQ3: What are the key components driving CHOIR’s success? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) validate CHOIR’s design, confirming that its success relies on several key components:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diverse Persona Paths are Essential:</span> The primary source of CHOIR’s strength is the complementary knowledge unlocked by different personas. When we remove this diversity by replacing the counterfactual paths with a single, repeated reasoning path (CHOIR w/ Same Reasoning Path), accuracy drops from 82.1% to 73.0%, confirming that the complementary knowledge from the different personas is important for performance gain.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T4.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:327.8pt;height:83.7pt;vertical-align:-39.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(19.6pt,-5.0pt) scale(1.13623652245745,1.13623652245745) ;">
<table id="S5.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CHOIR Components</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy (%)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">CHOIR (Full Method)</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">82.1</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ablations:</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">   w/ Same Reasoning Path (wo/ Diversity)</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">73.0</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">   w/ Random Personas (wo/ Structure)</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">80.3</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">   wo/ Pre-trained Knowledge</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">79.3</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><span id="S5.T4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation studies for CHOIR with Llama-8B on GSM8K (Gender).</span> Results show that CHOIR’s performance critically depends on: (1) leveraging <span id="S5.T4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">diverse reasoning paths</span>, (2) generating them through <span id="S5.T4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">structured counterfactual personas</span>, and (3) anchoring the ensemble with the model’s <span id="S5.T4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pre-trained knowledge</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
