<span class="ltx_bibblock">Automatic chain of thought prompting in large language models.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <em id="bib.bib34.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The Eleventh International Conference on Learning Representations</em>.

</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib35" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Zhao et al. (2024)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Jinman Zhao, Zifan Qian, Linbo Cao, Yining Wang, Yitian Ding, Yulan Hu, Zeyu Zhang, and Zeyong Jin. 2024.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.13979" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Role-play paradox in large language models: Reasoning performance gains and ethical dilemmas</a>.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><em id="bib.bib35.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Preprint</em>, arXiv:2409.13979.

</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib36" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Zheng et al. (2024)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Mingqian Zheng, Jiaxin Pei, Lajanugen Logeswaran, Moontae Lee, and David Jurgens. 2024.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><a href="https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.findings-emnlp.888" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">When “a helpful assistant” is not really helpful: Personas in system prompts do not improve performances of large language models</a>.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <em id="bib.bib36.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2024</em>, pages 15126–15154, Miami, Florida, USA. Association for Computational Linguistics.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p">Code and data are available at <a href="https://github.com/dongxiangjue/CHOIR" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/dongxiangjue/CHOIR</a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Extended Discussion and Future Directions</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has shown that persona-driven biases are subtle, persistent, and difficult to remove via simple prompt debiasing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al. (2024)</a></cite>. While much of the existing research treats this performance variation as a flaw to be mitigated, our work takes a fundamentally different approach. CHOIR does not attempt to suppress this variation; instead, it harnesses it, treating the divergent reasoning paths as complementary signals that can be fused into a more robust and accurate final answer. This reframing of sensitivity-as-a-resource is a key contribution of our work.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our use of minimal counterfactual edits (e.g., pronouns, age, disability terms) is to isolate the effect of demographic variation while preserving semantic consistency. This allowed us to: (1) validate that even minor shifts (e.g., “he” vs. “she”) can alter reasoning; (2) measure persona-induced variation without introducing semantic drift. Beyond counterfactual settings, our ablation results (Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.3 RQ3: What are the key components driving CHOIR’s success? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) further confirm that CHOIR’s benefits generalize: even randomly sampled personas (not just counterfactuals) improve performance, surpassing multiple strong baselines. This indicates that CHOIR’s robustness is not limited to carefully engineered demographic perturbations but extends to broader forms of reasoning diversity. For future work, extending the framework to richer or more nuanced persona dimensions (e.g., socio-political identity, cultural background, or domain expertise) may reveal additional layers of complementary reasoning, and we leave it to future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For confidence estimation, we employ maximum token probability as a practical proxy due to its simplicity, computational efficiency, and proven stability in prior work. Despite its lightweight nature, this measure consistently yields strong empirical gains. Since CHOIR operates during the final answer aggregation phase of Zero-Shot-CoT, token-level confidence provides a reliable signal for weighting reasoning paths. For future work, richer uncertainty quantification (e.g., sequence-level entropy, calibration-based measures, or cross-persona KL divergence) could further refine CHOIR’s weighting mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>More Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides additional details regarding the datasets, models, and prompting strategies used in our study. All experiments are conducted on NVIDIA A5000 24GB GPUs.</p>
</div>
