<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Comparison with Additional Baselines</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix D Comparison with Additional Baselines ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> provides a comprehensive comparison of CHOIR against a wider range of baseline methods on the GSM8K dataset using the Llama-8B model. These baselines include standard methods like Zero-Shot and Few-Shot prompting, as well as more advanced reasoning techniques like Role-Play prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> and Self-Consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022c</a>)</cite> under Zero-Shot CoT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Standard Zero-Shot prompting directly feeds the model a question without additional context, while Few-Shot prompting provides example inputs and outputs to guide responses. Zero-Shot CoT enhances reasoning by appending the phrase “Let’s think step by step,” encouraging step-by-step reasoning. Role-Play prompting with Zero-Shot CoT is similar to persona-assigned LLMs, prepending carefully crafted role descriptions to generate responses. Self-Consistency generates multiple reasoning paths and then selects the most consistent answer based on majority voting. For Self-Consistency, we set the sampling temperature to <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7</annotation></semantics></math> and generate three reasoning paths for fair comparisons.
From the table, we observe that among the baselines, Zero-Shot CoT improves accuracy from 15.5 to 73.2. However, Role-Play, which relies on predefined role prompts, does not generalize well to the math dataset. Notably, standard Self-Consistency with three reasoning paths performs poorly (22.1%), as the stochastic paths often diverge completely, leading to a high number of tied votes (1,278 tied problems) where no consensus can be reached. This trend aligns with findings from the original Self-Consistency paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022c</a>)</cite>, where using fewer sampled reasoning paths leads to even lower performance than greedy decoding (single-path). As CHOIR consistently surpasses all listed baselines by a substantial margin, we report only the strongest baselines in the main paper for clarity and focus.</p>
</div>
