<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Analysis of Computational Overhead</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CHOIR requires generating and processing <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> persona-conditioned reasoning paths for each input during inference. This comparison remains fair in the main paper, as strong baselines, Persona<sub id="A4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub">average</sub> and Persona<sub id="A4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub">majority</sub>, also aggregate <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> reasoning paths. In contrast, single-path methods like Zero-Shot, Few-Shot, Zero-Shot-CoT, and Role-Play generate only one reasoning path per query.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, the number of counterfactual personas varies by demographic attribute (Table <a href="#S1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>):
<math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=3</annotation></semantics></math> for Gender, Race, and Religion, and <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2</annotation></semantics></math> for Disability and Age.
Table <a href="#A4.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ D.1 Analysis of Computational Overhead ‣ Appendix D Comparison with Additional Baselines ‣ CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> presents the computational trade-off on GSM8K using LLaMA3-8B-Instruct, comparing CHOIR with both single-path and multi-path baselines in terms of accuracy, latency, and relative efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">CHOIR increases inference latency by approximately <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2-3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2-3\times</annotation></semantics></math> relative to single-path methods (e.g., Zero-Shot-CoT), as expected from processing multiple reasoning paths. However, it achieves a <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.07-1.12\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.07</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1.12</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.07-1.12\times</annotation></semantics></math> relative improvement, demonstrating a strong performance-efficiency trade-off.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">When compared to multi-path baselines (e.g., Persona<sub id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_sub">majority</sub>), CHOIR attains substantially higher accuracy than baselines at identical computational cost. For <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=3</annotation></semantics></math>, CHOIR is 2.7 points more accurate with no additional latency, underscoring the effectiveness of its dynamic weighting mechanism over static aggregation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical Considerations.</span>
In practical deployments, such as medical QA, legal reasoning, or policy analysis, where reasoning reliability outweighs minor latency increases, this trade-off is both justified and beneficial. Future work can explore optimizing CHOIR’s efficiency. For instance, adaptive techniques such as “early exiting”, where computation halts once personas reach a strong consensus, or “persona pruning”, where only a diverse subset of personas are used for a given query, could reduce latency without sacrificing significant accuracy.</p>
</div>
